The two Gemini 3.1 Pro rows no longer under-bill. /cost's per-turn breakdown now prices a prompt over 200K tokens at Google's higher rate, using the tier code that was already there. It's committed as 967cbc65, pushed, and I commented on #1012 naming both rows and the sha. Build, all 25 test binaries, clippy -D warnings and fmt --check pass.

What changed (`src/format/cost.rs`):
• A new helper, yoyo_side_tier_cost, covers gemini-3.1-pro-preview and -customtools. It holds the base rates ($2 / $0.20 cache read / $12) plus Google's over-200K rates ($4 / $0.40 / $18).
• estimate_request_cost_with uses this helper only when no yoagent preset has tiers for the model.
• A user's [model_pricing] entry still replaces the price entirely, with no tier.
• The "not modelled" comment is gone. The file is at 3602 lines against a 3605 cap.

Tests (`context_tier_tests.rs`), with the expected costs worked out by hand from Google's rates:
• A 250K prompt is billed at the higher rate on both ids.
• Exactly 200K stays at the base rate (the tier only applies strictly above).
• gemini-3-pro-preview, which has no tier, and a user price override both stay at the flat rate.

Positive control: my first attempt at breaking the code was too broad (it also broke the existing Haiku tests), and my second edit didn't apply, so that run proved nothing. The third made the helper match no model id. Only the 250K test failed, the boundary tests stayed green, and everything passed again once restored.

Docs (`cost-tracking.md`):
• The Gemini 3.1 Pro sentence now describes the real behaviour.
• "Haiku 5.5 is the only model yoyo prices with a tier" was no longer true. I replaced it with a pointer rather than a new "only" claim, because yoagent's bundled price data may give other presets tiers I haven't checked.
• I added the policy line the task asked for: where a vendor's price varies, /cost either models the variation or uses the higher rate. I didn't write "never reads low", because two places still do, and both are named in the policy as exceptions, not fixed:
  • DeepSeek is priced at its off-peak rate.
  • For models with a tier (Haiku 5.5, Gemini 3.1 Pro), the session total line uses the base rate; only the per-turn breakdown applies the tier.

These two are the only other rows that read low; I listed them in the commit body as the task asked.
